# Lane b6_design: which grounding module goes into the next training leg, and what exactly is built (inventory and design; read-only, no build)

## Why
The project trains a 9B spatial vision-language model that answers from RGB end to end. For routed question types the model calls its own perception: a \`<ground>\` call must find the named object in the frames. Grounding is the measured bottleneck: measurement programs score 80.1 with ground-truth perception, 58.7 with the model's own heads, 73.0 when the model answers directly; of 1,056 operand instances 224 are never proposed by the deployed segmenter, and wrong accepted objects cost 12 task points on relative distance. The next leg (the "repair seal") is to stack the best method pieces, and the planning report (\`$R/next_queue/out/NEXT_QUEUE_20261115.md\`, build brief **B6**, section 1.6, candidates M1 to M4 in section 2.4) calls for an own-encoder grounding head inside the seal, labelled a guess. The user's rulings: a frozen SAM 3 tower inside the model is allowed; the ideal is grounding from the model's own vision encoder; fine-tune or swap encoders, never train one from scratch; no external harness may choose an answer; any init or data source is checked against benchmark scenes first.

Several grounding pieces were built and measured over the last two weeks, and nobody has put them side by side. Before a builder spends effort, I need a design.

## Part 1: inventory (cite path and line or file for every number)
For each grounding piece that exists, state what it is, what was measured, on which scenes, whether its weights and kit still exist and where, and whether its training scenes exclude the benchmark and held-out development scenes: at least the lanes \`own_ground\`, \`own_probe\`, \`own_head\`, \`own_teach\`, \`own_reread\`, \`grd_selector\`, \`mask_recall\`, \`mdg_build\`, \`mdg_next\`, \`gt_ceiling\`, \`sam3_tower\`, \`hrp_infer\`, \`hrp_rows\`, \`box3d_probe\`, \`verify_kit\` under \`$R/\` (read each lane's STATUS.md and the out/ reports it names; skip a lane that does not exist), the design records listed in \`/data2/jjyeung/agent_project_data/distill_design/INDEX.md\` on grounding, presence, pointing and masks, and the memory notes \`result-presence-pose-routing-20261110.md\`, \`user-ruling-sam3-tower-own-encoder-20261110.md\`, \`mdga-init-ineligible-20261113.md\`, \`pointing-masks-lost-on-aws-20261112.md\`, \`result-devs1-route-table-predicted-masks-20261112.md\` under \`/home/jjyeung/.claude/projects/-scratch-jjyeung-orch-home-distill/memory/\` (single named files only).

## Part 2: design
Recommend ONE grounding design for the seal, with the losers rejected for stated reasons. Cover at least: (a) the frozen SAM 3 tower prompted by the model's own pointing or presence signal; (b) a mask or box head on the model's own visual features; (c) the existing selector over segmenter proposals with a learned verifier; (d) no new module, only training the routed rows on deployed-pipeline responses so the model learns when not to trust a grounding (a separate lane, \`$R/trace_ruling\`, is ruling on that; read its \`out/TRACE_RULING_20261115.md\` if it exists). For the recommendation give: the module and where it sits in the forward pass; what is trained, frozen and initialized from what, with the scene-eligibility evidence; the supervision and where it comes from (which masks or boxes survive, which were lost); how its output reaches the language model as text tokens; the expected gain priced in points of the official benchmarks with the arithmetic, or the word "guess"; the cheapest test on training-side scenes that would kill it before a full leg; the CPU proofs a builder must deliver; memory and time cost per training step.
Then write the build brief a builder lane can execute without asking questions (scope, inputs with paths, files it may touch, CPU proofs, deliverables).

## Rules
- Read-only everywhere except your lane folder. No GPU, no launch, no ssh, no agents, no Git commands, no code build beyond small read scripts. Python: \`/scratch/jjyeung/envs/p1cpu/bin/python -I -s -B\`.
- Never list or search \`/home\` or \`/data*\`; read only the single files named here and the records the index names. No recursive search outside the lane folders named here under \`/scratch/jjyeung/orch_cd\`. Never read a file over 20 MB whole.
- No deletion commands, and no names of deletion operations in shell command text.
- Where the evidence does not settle something, say what is known and what is not; label guesses. Do not soften a finding.

## Deliver
\`out/B6_GROUNDING_DESIGN_20261115.md\`, written as a design record (question, answer, reasons with evidence, options rejected with reasons, what might not work and how we would notice, tests that would settle it, status), ending with the build brief. One event \`events/<stamp>_RESULT_design.md\` (stamp from \`date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%MZ\` in the writing command), at most 40 lines, when the file is complete. Then stay alive 30 minutes checking \`inbox/\` once a minute.
